# Build output
/bin/
/dist/
*.exe

# `go build ./tools/<x>` and `go build ./cmd/<x>` drop the binary in the working
# directory under the package's own name, which is how one of these was
# committed once — and then how a 3.3 MB `check-interface` was committed a
# second time, because this list is a list of names and a name that is not on it
# is not ignored.
#
# THE LIST IS NO LONGER THE DEFENCE. `internal/repository_test.go` reads the
# tracked files and fails on anything that starts with an executable's magic
# bytes, whatever it is called. These lines stay because keeping the working
# directory clean is worth something on its own, and because a `git status` that
# is quiet is one somebody reads.
/analyse
/api
/bundle
/check-css
/check-interface
/fonts
/load
/migrate
/release
/reset
/restore-drill
/seed
/settle
/staff
/validate-content
/world

# Go
*.test
*.out
/vendor/

# The offline bundle. It is one school at one moment, written by `tools/bundle`
# from a running server — committing one would be committing a snapshot of a
# database, and it would be wrong the day after.
/bundle.html
/*.bundle.html

# Node — package.json and its lock ARE committed: the browser suites pin the
# driver they were tested against, and a suite that silently upgraded it would
# fail on a day nobody changed anything. What is not committed is what npm
# unpacks from them.
/node_modules/
npm-debug.log*

# Terraform — state never belongs in the repository; it lives in a bucket,
# versioned and locked. A .tfvars file usually carries values that are not
# meant to be read by anybody who clones this.
.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfvars
crash.log

# Local environment
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Editors and operating systems
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
